Date: Sat, 11 Dec 93 10:27:11 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Bear Essentials

                     *** for the line-eater ***

    In a zoology class, the professor recently described a dangerous
encounter between a field entomologist and a mother grizzly bear with
cubs.  I described the incident to a couple of friends, and the
question arose of whether it would have been a practical strategy for
the entomologist to have been carrying a weapon (or to generalize
slightly, any kind of bear-deterring device), and if so, what.  I had
no idea, but I thought the question might be of interest to some of
the folks here.  Relevant data may include...

    (1)  The encounter was at close range and unexpected.  The two
species took one another by surprise in thick cover, at a range that
may have been as little as 10 meters.  Such encounters are among the
most to be feared with bears, since at longer ranges and with more
warning, the two species will probably choose to avoid one another.
    (2)  Bears are fast.  Over short distances, grizzlies can run about
twice as rapidly as humans, especially on rough terrain.
    (3)  Since the grizzly was defending cubs, an attempt to scare her
might have been counterproductive.
    (4)  The entomologist was alone.  No budget for bodyguards.

    The discussions about weapons have run as follows:  Clearly, a
weapon would have to provide an extremely quick kill or disable, for a
hit that took even as little as a few seconds to do so might allow
sufficient time for the angry, wounded bear to take the human with it.
Yet it seemed to us (who were not particularly into weaponry) that any
such weapon would likely be large and clumsy, and would have to be
carried at the ready, and even so might require a good deal of luck or
skill to be used effectively against a charging bear.  Furthermore,
the circumstances in question were not those of a hunt or a combat
patrol:  The human was doing work which required him to be carrying
other gear, the use of which often required that his hands and arms be
unencumbered.

    One thought was something on the lines of a can of Mace.

    If one were willing to take the risk of scaring the bear, there
are probably lots of noise- and light-makers that one could use to do
so.

    A bear-escaping device would also be a solution to the problem,
but I did not know of any source of seven-league boots or backpack
JATO units.  (I should mention that this particular encounter took
place near timber line, where such trees as there were were only about
six feet tall.  Climbing one would only have served to bring the human
up to bear eye level.)

                                    -- Jay Freeman,
                                       who does not always support
                                       the right to arm bears

