From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Brave New Extropia? No Thanks.

I'm still mulling over this discussion but wanted to get my vote in
before it got too cold.

I agree with Amara that TRANShumanism leaves me cold. I think that we
are all human, and I share with the list a deep interest in where we 
might be evolving to, but I am always conscious of the distance
between here and there, and the work that is necessary to get there.
We live in a world where much of what happens seems to display no
rational pattern, where emotion, or instinct, or blind impulse seem to
be the motivators (I just erased my attempt to write that sentence in
E-prime because it was four times as long and said less)
  The most distressing conflict I ever have had with a list member
came when I heard him drawing lines between "mere humans" and himself,
and when pressed, he said he no longer considered himself human. Well,
wishing doesn't make it so. 
  
 Also, this list has very rigid rules; newcomers are challenged,
criticized, and sometimes ridiculed if they do not conform.
Personally, I can handle that sort of environment; I learned to be a
Unix System Administrator by insisting that I be allowed into the
game. It's a dynamic similar to a boy's ball game, where little boys
are chased away and told to come back when they are big; they are
allowed in the game if they demonstrate skill, and persistence, and
maybe if they show up with a ball when no-one else has one, and they
are laughed at, but respected for coming back and trying again.

  Many women have no idea how to handle this sort of situation. The
book "Games Mother Never Taught You" was written almost 20 years ago,
and yet many women still haven't learned that competition is not a
personal attack. While the boys are out on the ball field learning
about pecking orders and game playing, the girls are playing house,
playing cooperation games like jacks and jumping rope, and playing
social games involving groups and relationships. (Betty Lehman Harrigan)
I still give this book to my friends (I should footnote that I've met
a  couple of men who don't understand this either, but they don't seem
to stay in the game at all.) 
    
   One thing I've noticed about this sort of competitive situation is that
once it's pointed out, many women think the rules should be changed,
while many men think the women should get into the game. In terms of
this list, the list rules seem very fixed. Take it or leave it (or 
develop your own PPL, but that's within the larger fixed framework.)

BTW I particularly enjoyed Steve's analysis. In my ideal reality, men
and women would learn the best of each other's skills...men would 
look at their feelings and motivations, and form emotional friendships
(at least with women; maybe with each other they'd still just hang)
and women would sharpen their skills against the market, honing their
abilities to become all that they are capable of becoming, but without
meanness or spite...
 
In a woman's dream of Utopia, less is written about the technology,
and more on how we fill our lives with meaning and warmth ...









